he parts of the antibody  that binds the antigen and  the resulting antibodies have improved  affinity  and upon the second or third exposure to  the same antigen  these whole cycles go on and on until  a very high affinity antibodies are  reached um  mainly in the uh reaching  stealing affinity of the of the in the  nanowire  range so in our group  we isolate hyafini antibodies that  enable specific neutralization  and successive detection of target  antigens  mostly toxins viral cells and bacteria  and our methodology is to obtain  peripheral subcultural lymphocytes  from human patients or from immunized  animals  and then we isolate hyaphine antibodies  using fresh display  libraries we generate a  full-length antibodies and characterize  them  just a quick overview of our methodology  so this is the antibody we are talking  about the viability  a that is expressed as a single chain  both the vehicle and the vehicle aspects  of the sync engine  and a antibody on the surface of a  filamentous  phage the enthalpy in phage we create  a library that contains hundreds of  million different phages or different  antibodies and then by process  termed panning we select  only these antibodies that specifically  bind the antigen this process can be  repeated several times  in order to enrich  and specifically isolate the desired  antibodies  the way to develop a or to create  such a good library is to amplify  the genes that encodes to the whole  variety of antibodies from the donor  both to the heavy chain a viable  religion and to the light and viable  legends we are talking about  both the lambta and the kappa in order  to do this  we have a specific and very  um large repertoire of primers for each  specie we then  assemble them randomly together to  create  two libraries one for the heavy train  with the lambda  a trains and the second for the copper  chains  and then we sequence many clones  of each library in order to ensure that  it is  a a diverse we we're using  the sanger sequencing stack studio  um system and just for example after a  second round of planning  we we test a random  um clones from the library and as you  can see  there are several clones in each library  that bind  to the antigen and after the third round  most of the clones usually bind the  antigen  some of them with a very high affinity  we then reformat the antibodies for  to a full length a igg so  this is the an antibody so we are using  for all our antibodies a constant  region a human fc igg one  type a region  to which we join the viable regions that  were derived from the  phase display library at the end of the  planning process  we then expressed these antibodies intro  or in heck two nine three cells here we  find them in  further characterize basically  in our laboratory we are focusing on  a on by pathogens  that are a biplate associated  but in the last few months we have  isolated a panel of human antibodies  that we hope will serve as the basis for  combined post-exposure therapy for scope  to  infection so star scope 2 this is the  virus you are  all well familiar with this we are  specifically want to target the spikes  and what we aim to do is to  isolate antibodies that target different  epitopes  on the spike protein so  we obtained the lymphocytes from a  recovered  impatience we also obtained from a  humanized animals but today i will  focus on our human  antibodies we created the library  and now we are characterizing using the  antibodies  so we have found the rivalry using  the receptor binding domain this is the  the moiety  of the spikes that specifically bind  the human h2 receptor  and we cloned the the resulted  we sequenced the resulted clones uh  reformatted them into a igg  and by the end of this project we ended  up  with eight different antibodies that  belong  to a different germ lines both for the  heavy  and for the lychees  this antibodies specifically bind  to the receptor binding domain which is  part of the  s1 uh region of the spike  as you can see it does not by that they  are  not binding the antenna domain for  example  we then wanted to measure  their affinity so we are using octet  it's a bio-layer  interferometry system the antibody in  question  is immobilized to the sensor  and then we add the the  the antigen in this case rbd a different  concentration  and we measure both the k on and the k  off  part and and then we can calculate for  each antibody  the the the the affinity the the kd  and as can be seen here all our  antibodies  have a quite good affinity which is in  the lower  and even sub and anamola range  we also asked whether our antibodies can  bind  the antigens simultaneously or in other  words  whether they bind to different epitopes  so we again are using the octet system  so the first antibody in question for  example fd65  is immobilized on the sensor and then we  add  the the antigen in this case the s1  and as you can see upon binding of the  s1 there is a very nice  a shift of indicating for  the binding to the antibody and then we  wash the  the axis and then we add another  antibody  so if the second antibody binds to  different  epitope on the antigen then it will  result  in another wavelength shift as shown  here  if it's bind to the same epitope  then we will have no a further a  shift so we characterized  all the antibodies and  we could classify them into four  groups two of the groups  only are represented only by one  antibody  and two groups have three antibodies  and basically what we are  seeing here is that the the small  moiety the receptor binding domain  contains  at least four different epitopes  we then asked whether those antibodies  can neutralize  the the virus we are using in vitro  system  to test the neutralization uh on a  on a various cells and as can be seen  here basically  seven out of the eight antibodies  can can neutralize the virus we have  also  added here a control antibody the rb18  and isotype control  and just want you to focus on our best  uh neutralization a neutralizing  antibody  md65 which belongs to the second group  and also you can see that the antibody  62 from group 3  is also a a very  high neutralization potency  we then ask whether we can say something  about  the mechanism of the neutralization of  these antibodies  again using the octet so the first  antibody  is immobilized the sensor and then we  add  the receptor binding domain and then we  add  the receptor a recombinant receptor  in human a2 and we test  when we test whether this receptor can  bind  to the rbd in the presence of the  neutralizing antibody  so we took a represent a representative  antibody from each group  and as can clearly be seen here  is that for example antibody 29 belongs  to group  1 does not inhibit  the binding of the receptor to the rbd  and so does antibody 47  while two of our best neutralizing  antibodies  antibodies 65 and antibody 62  completely abolished the ability of the  receptor to bind to the ability  so this can tell us about  the most important maybe most important  part  of the bi of the of the neutralization a  mechanism needed for a star scope too i  will  please bear in mind that antibodies  47 and 29 both  inhibit the activity of the virus in  vitro  so i guess maybe  probably to other important  mechanisms  so where we are now so we are continuing  to characterize the activity of the  antibodies  in vivo in animal models we also  perform clinical evaluations both  pharmacokinetics  toxicity etc we are currently also  characterize another set of human  antibodies  and antibodies that have been derived  from a  saskov to immunize the animals  and with that i would like to thank the  people in my group  and all the other people who helped us  to the anti-activated engineering  group and to our colleagues from their  biology unit  and thank you all for listening  thank you dr mazur for that outstanding  presentation  and we will now move to the live q a  por